<div>
<p>Learning to evaluate philosophical writing before you produce it</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>This page contains full instructions for the Paper Preparation Exercise, including the term paper rubric used to evaluate all papers in this course. Read it carefully before you begin.</p>
</div></div>
<h3>Why this exercise exists</h3>
<p>Writing a philosophy paper is a specific skill, and one of the best ways to develop it is to evaluate philosophical writing before you attempt it yourself. This exercise gives you that opportunity. You will read three sample papers written in response to a Unit 1 prompt, assess each one using the same rubric that will be used to grade your own term paper, and write a short evaluative report.</p>
<p>The goal is not simply to rank the papers. It is to develop a clear sense of what distinguishes a strong philosophical argument from a weak one — so that when you write your own paper, you know what you are aiming for.</p>
<h3>The sample papers</h3>
<p>The three papers below were written in response to the following prompt — which is a Unit 1 topic, not your assignment:</p>
<div>
<p>Original prompt (not your assignment)</p>
<p>In Plato’s <em>Apology</em>, Socrates informs the jury that he would defy their orders to cease practicing philosophy if they were to release him. Contrarily, in Plato’s <em>Crito</em>, Socrates asserts that one should never disobey their city. Your task is to develop and defend a thesis addressing this seeming contradiction. If you believe the two positions can be reconciled, provide textual evidence to support your claim. If you argue that one passage more accurately represents Plato’s views, explain why the other passage should be given less weight. If you think Plato is genuinely contradicting himself, help us comprehend the reason behind this contradiction. Do not assume that your reader will automatically concur with your assertions.</p>
</div>
<p>Read each paper in full before evaluating any of them. Your evaluation will be stronger if you have a sense of the range of quality across all three before you start writing.</p>
<div><span>📄 &nbsp; <a title="Paper #1" href="$WIKI_REFERENCE$/pages/g60c9b7eaa4350757d2a4e2239545502d" data-course-type="wikiPages" data-published="true">Paper #1 — “Obey the gods”</a></span></div>
<div><span>📄 &nbsp; <a title="Paper #2" href="$WIKI_REFERENCE$/pages/g56a3a452dcc32c5a6dda2886eea25edb" data-course-type="wikiPages" data-published="true">Paper #2 — “Plato Paper”</a></span></div>
<div><span>📄 &nbsp; <a title="Paper #3" href="$WIKI_REFERENCE$/pages/gbe1e9738f2e5a394532ee0c6e901bf74" data-course-type="wikiPages" data-published="true">Paper #3 — “Obey the law or persuade the state”</a></span></div>
<h3>Step-by-step instructions</h3>
<div>
<div>① &nbsp; Read all three sample papers.</div>
<div>② &nbsp; Evaluate each paper using the rubric below. Assign a letter grade to each one.</div>
<div>③ &nbsp; For each paper, write one paragraph identifying its <strong>strengths</strong> and one paragraph identifying its <strong>weaknesses</strong>.</div>
<div>④ &nbsp; Submit your report — six paragraphs total, approximately 750–1000 words — on the <strong>Paper Preparation Exercise</strong> assignment page.</div>
</div>
<div class="callout callout--connection"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">➡️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Required</p>
<p>You must assign a letter grade to each paper based on the rubric. A report without grades will be considered incomplete.</p>
</div></div>
<h3>Term Paper Rubric</h3>
<p>This is the same rubric used to grade your own term paper. Use it to evaluate the three sample papers, and keep it in mind when you write.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Category</th>
<th>Description</th>
<th>Weight</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Details</td>
<td>A meaningful title, student name, course number, professor’s name, date, page numbers, and a works cited section. References follow MLA style. Under five pages of double-spaced text (no minimum length).</td>
<td>10%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Assignment</td>
<td>Does the paper actually fulfill the assignment?</td>
<td>10%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Audience</td>
<td>Written for members of this class who have already read the text carefully. Does not assume the reader already <strong>agrees</strong> with the paper.</td>
<td>5%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Introduction</td>
<td>Avoids sweeping generalizations. Indicates what the paper is about and excites the reader’s interest. States a thesis the paper will defend. Optionally provides a brief map of the argument.</td>
<td>10%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Thesis</td>
<td>Clear but not obvious to someone who has already read the text. Appears near the beginning of the paper, preferably in the first paragraph. May seem surprising until the reader has been convinced by the argument.</td>
<td>15%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Topic sentences</td>
<td>Each paragraph should ideally begin with a topic sentence that offers support to the thesis. Taken together, the topic sentences should form a rough summary of the paper’s argument.</td>
<td>5%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Evidence</td>
<td>Points are backed up with evidence from the text. Evidence actually supports the point being made. <strong>If you can complete this assignment without detailed textual evidence, you are approaching it too superficially.</strong></td>
<td>20%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Avoiding unnecessary summaries</td>
<td>Since the audience is already familiar with the text, the paper should not merely summarize it. Focus the reader’s attention on aspects of the text that support the thesis.</td>
<td>15%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Conclusion</td>
<td>In a short paper, the conclusion should be more than a repetition of what the reader has already read. Ideally it draws broader implications, raises new ideas, or poses thoughtful questions.</td>
<td>10%</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
